<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fruela II of Asturias</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 875–925</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Fruela II of León</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Fruela II was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturias">Asturias</a> from 910 and of León from 924 until his death in 925. A son of Alfonso III the Great, he received Asturias when his father's realm was divided among his sons, and on the death of his brother Ordoño II he took the throne of León, reuniting the inheritance. His short reign was followed by a succession dispute among his nephews and sons.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fruela-ii-of-asturias.jpg" alt="Fruela II of Asturias" width="273" height="314" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fruela2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Fruela was a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-iii-of-asturias">Alfonso III of Asturias</a> and his wife Jimena. In 910 he and two of his brothers deposed their father and divided the kingdom among themselves. Fruela, the youngest brother, took the original portion, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturias">Asturias</a>; Ordoño took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a>; and García, the eldest, took León. Although Fruela called himself king of a much-diminished Asturias, evidence suggests that his role was subordinate to the king of León. As king of Asturias he had the task of consolidating the region later called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> and keeping its counts in check.</p><p>Fruela married twice, first in 910 to Nunilo Jimena, daughter of the Navarrese king, and by 917 to Urraca, daughter of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-qasi">Banu Qasi</a> governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tudela">Tudela</a>. He had at least three sons, Alfonso, Ramiro and Ordoño, none of whom inherited the throne. Bishop Pelayo gives all three to Nunila, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-khaldun">Ibn Khaldun</a> gives Ordoño and Ramiro to Urraca.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Fruela kept good relations with his brother Ordoño, who held the hegemony. They cooperated in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reconquista">Reconquista</a>, and Fruela signed Ordoño's diplomas as Froila frater confirmat, 'Fruela the brother confirms'. When Ordoño died in 924 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnates</a> ignored his heirs and elected Fruela king. He had never been popular with the nobles and his subjects, and some doubt that his election was regular and see it as a likely usurpation.</p><p>He had Gebuldo and Aresindo, sons of Olmundo, who claimed descent from King Witiza, assassinated, which alienated the nobility further. One chronicler relates that for this he was condemned to a reign of only fourteen months. According to Ramón Menéndez Pidal, he also exiled Frunimio, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of León and a relation of Olmundo. He reigned for fourteen months and died of leprosy in August 925.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After Fruela's death several claimants competed for his lands. They included Ramiro, who appears to have married the widowed queen Urraca and used the royal title but was eventually unsuccessful, the sons of Ordoño II and Fruela's own young sons. There is some debate about the immediate succession.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Fruela II of Asturias” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The realm was divided among his sons: García I took León, the new centre of royal power, Ordoño II took Galicia, and <b>Fruela II</b> took Asturias itself.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-asturias">Kingdom of Asturias · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>García I took the lands south of the mountains, centred on León, Ordoño received Galicia and <b>Fruela</b> Asturias.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-leon">Kingdom of León · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oviedo">Oviedo</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in León Province, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.36,-5.85&amp;year=907" aria-label="Oviedo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4409 625 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Fruela II of Asturias"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4859 895h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4859 895h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.36,-5.85&amp;year=907">What was here in 907 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
